Today I built and verified a secure AWS setup where an EC2 server runs a web application that connects to a MySQL database in RDS. The EC2 instance uses an IAM role to read database credentials from AWS Secrets Manager instead of storing passwords in code. The database is private and only allows access from the EC2 security group on port 3306. I tested the application, fixed IAM and security group issues, ran validation scripts to confirm the setup was correct, and collected the required screenshots and audit files. I then uploaded the work to a shared GitHub repository and secured it by removing sensitive logs and adding rules to prevent keys or secrets from being committed.\
Resume version:\
Built an EC2 to RDS MySQL application using IAM roles and Secrets Manager to handle database credentials securely. Configured security groups so the database is private and only accessible from the application server on port 3306. Validated the environment with AWS CLI checks and fixed misconfigurations until all checks passed. Generated audit evidence and safely pushed the lab deliverables to a shared GitHub repository after removing sensitive information.\
One sentence:\
I set up, verified, and documented a secure EC2 to RDS application using IAM roles, Secrets Manager, and restricted network access, then safely shared the results in GitHub.\
